WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:04.700
When you think of your favorite band or musician,
what image comes to mind?

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:09.740
For a lot of people, it’s fancy cars, Gucci
jewelry, and a mansion in Beverly Hills.

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:14.870
But this kind of lifestyle is becoming less
and less common, even for artists on major

00:00:14.870 --> 00:00:15.870
labels.

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:22.010
The median musician makes about $35,000 a
year, and not all of that comes from music.

00:00:22.010 --> 00:00:27.160
A lot of artists need other gigs, and even
the ones who can survive solely on music are

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:28.250
often middle-class.

00:00:28.250 --> 00:00:34.120
With less people buying music and artists
making very little from streaming, the bulk

00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:37.160
of a musician’s income comes from playing
live shows.

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:42.920
But because the Covid-19 pandemic is preventing
these live shows from happening, many musicians

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:45.150
are more strapped for cash than ever.

00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:49.010
For most musicians, it’s a constant hustle,
with little return.

00:00:49.010 --> 00:00:52.930
This is why Julia and I decided not to pursue
this career ourselves.

00:00:52.930 --> 00:00:54.050
Even though I love to play guitar…

00:00:54.050 --> 00:00:55.180
...and I love to sing...

00:00:55.180 --> 00:01:00.140
...we just couldn’t see how a career in
music would be financially sustainable.

00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:02.760
But we don’t want to see other musicians
throw in the towel.

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:05.059
Their music is too important.

00:01:05.059 --> 00:01:09.149
It helps us enjoy life, feel moved, and see
things from new angles.

00:01:09.149 --> 00:01:15.000
So is there something fans can do to help
or is a music career a thing of the past?

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:30.170
Popular music was more or less invented in
the early 20th century, thanks to two technological

00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:35.429
innovations: radio, which allowed people to
discover new sounds, and the phonograph, which

00:01:35.429 --> 00:01:38.929
allowed musicians to monetize their songs
by selling copies.

00:01:38.929 --> 00:01:44.119
Though the physical format has changed over
the years, the basic system lasted for most

00:01:44.119 --> 00:01:49.299
of the century, giving rise to hundreds of
famous artists, from Nat King Cole to Britney

00:01:49.299 --> 00:01:53.969
Spears, and spawning several multi-billion
dollar record labels.

00:01:53.969 --> 00:01:58.770
This business model was upended in the late
'90s, with the invention of an audio codec

00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:03.149
called the mp3, and a file-sharing program
called Napster.

00:02:03.149 --> 00:02:08.929
Suddenly, songs could be encoded into very
small file sizes, and Napster let users share

00:02:08.929 --> 00:02:10.390
them with each other for free.

00:02:10.390 --> 00:02:14.459
All you had to do was search the song or artist
you wanted to hear and you’d be connected

00:02:14.459 --> 00:02:16.580
to a user who had that music.

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:21.689
For the first time ever, listeners had an
entire world of music at their fingertips.

00:02:21.689 --> 00:02:26.650
It was awesome for them…not so much for
the musicians, who felt like their work was

00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:29.900
being stolen—which, well, it kind of was.

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:34.519
The major record labels weren’t so happy
about it either, since Napster wasn’t paying

00:02:34.519 --> 00:02:35.969
them royalties.

00:02:35.969 --> 00:02:40.860
They came after the company with copyright
lawsuits and won, causing Napster to declare

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:42.450
bankruptcy in 2002.

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:45.099
But the genie was already out of the bottle.

00:02:45.099 --> 00:02:49.800
New file-sharing programs popped up to fill
the void left by Napster, and though there

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:54.719
were legitimate ways to digitally purchase
songs, like Apple’s new iTunes platform,

00:02:54.719 --> 00:02:57.390
it’s really hard to compete with free.

00:02:57.390 --> 00:03:01.829
The industry struggled to monetize, and music
sales plummeted in the 2000s.

00:03:01.829 --> 00:03:06.269
Some economists describe this as a “categorical
change.”

00:03:06.269 --> 00:03:11.680
Psychologically, consumers had “categorized”
music as something that you didn’t pay for,

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:14.890
like emails or tortilla chips at a Mexican
restaurant.

00:03:14.890 --> 00:03:19.110
Categorical changes are really hard to reverse,
once they’ve taken hold.

00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:23.799
But the following decade, the music industry
figured out how to get people to pay for music

00:03:23.799 --> 00:03:28.879
without feeling like they were paying for
music: subscription streaming services, like

00:03:28.879 --> 00:03:30.640
Pandora and Spotify.

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:36.150
For a flat monthly fee, subscribers can listen
to all the music they want, and the platform

00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:39.060
pays royalties each time a song is played.

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:44.379
These royalties are split between songwriters,
publishers, record labels, recording artists,

00:03:44.379 --> 00:03:45.540
and managers.

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:47.390
Geez...talk about a lot of middlemen!

00:03:47.390 --> 00:03:52.201
Even though these royalties make up 80 percent
of the music industry’s revenue, the musicians

00:03:52.201 --> 00:03:54.920
themselves tend to make very little.

00:03:54.920 --> 00:04:05.069
In 2019, artists earned on average 0.00437
cents on Spotify and 0.0168 cents on Pandora.

00:04:05.069 --> 00:04:07.730
Other streaming services weren’t much better.

00:04:07.730 --> 00:04:11.639
These rates aren’t set in stone, though,
and vary by artist.

00:04:11.639 --> 00:04:16.320
That’s because streaming services usually
take company earnings and divide them up based

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:19.810
on how many streams a particular artist has.

00:04:19.810 --> 00:04:26.000
So if you pay $9.99 a month and only listen
to one obscure indie band, in a perfect world,

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:29.410
the entire ten bucks would go straight to
that band.

00:04:29.410 --> 00:04:31.950
But that’s sadly not how it works.

00:04:31.950 --> 00:04:34.760
The money is split by every artist.

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:36.000
And not equally, either.

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:42.230
If, say, Billie Eilish gets 10 percent of
all streams on Spotify, she’ll get 10 percent

00:04:42.230 --> 00:04:45.910
of your money, even though you never listened
to her.

00:04:45.910 --> 00:04:50.500
The more streams an artist has, the bigger
piece of the pie they get.

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:54.990
I wish we could say this money adds up to
a pretty hefty paycheck, but that’s not

00:04:54.990 --> 00:04:56.930
usually the case.

00:04:56.930 --> 00:05:01.710
Musicians would need about 87,000 plays a
month on Pandora to earn the national minimum

00:05:01.710 --> 00:05:05.980
wage, and about 336,000 plays on Spotify.

00:05:05.980 --> 00:05:10.440
Even if you manage to get millions of streams,
it’s still not a big payout.

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:15.320
So if streaming services aren’t typically
lucrative and you can’t go to live shows

00:05:15.320 --> 00:05:18.150
right now, how do you support your favorite
musicians?

00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:20.780
Thankfully, there are lots of ways.

00:05:20.780 --> 00:05:24.620
You could pull a 1999 and buy their music.

00:05:24.620 --> 00:05:29.060
Artists signed to major record labels often
make 10 to 15 percent off these sales, and

00:05:29.060 --> 00:05:30.670
indie artists earn even more.

00:05:30.670 --> 00:05:35.140
So, if you buy their album for $10, they’ll
make at least a buck.

00:05:35.140 --> 00:05:39.340
But, what if the album is already on a subscription
service you use, like Spotify?

00:05:39.340 --> 00:05:42.270
In that case, you might consider buying their
merch.

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:46.780
This could be a t-shirt, hoodie, perfume,
or...a doormat?

00:05:46.780 --> 00:05:49.740
Man, Swifties aren’t messing around!

00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:54.950
Artists who run their own merch can take home
most or all of the money they make from those

00:05:54.950 --> 00:05:55.950
sales.

00:05:55.950 --> 00:05:59.300
Other artists typically earn around 30 percent
of the merch sale, depending on the deal they

00:05:59.300 --> 00:06:01.070
have with the merchandising company.

00:06:01.070 --> 00:06:05.840
If you’d rather support your favorite musicians
directly, see if they have a Patreon, which

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:11.140
lets fans pay creators every month in return
for cool rewards like live performances and

00:06:11.140 --> 00:06:12.360
unreleased music.

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:17.430
Some artists are even streaming live on Instagram,
Facebook, Youtube, and Twitch, which may allow

00:06:17.430 --> 00:06:18.950
you to tip them.

00:06:18.950 --> 00:06:23.780
Or maybe they’re playing full-blown digital
concerts on StageIt, where you can buy tickets.

00:06:23.780 --> 00:06:28.850
Soundcloud even lets you send money directly
to their Paypal, Venmo, or Cash App.

00:06:28.850 --> 00:06:33.040
You could also support musicians through good
ol’ fashioned word-of-mouth.

00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:34.800
Post about the band on Facebook.

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:36.610
Make your roommate listen to them.

00:06:36.610 --> 00:06:40.260
This gives them new listeners, who could turn
into fans and supporters.

00:06:40.260 --> 00:06:44.530
Plus, the more buzz a band gets, the more
likely they’ll catch the attention of filmmakers

00:06:44.530 --> 00:06:48.040
and advertisers for those sweet licensing
deals.

00:06:50.050 --> 00:06:55.120
However you choose to support musicians, we
suggest going beyond streaming services.

00:06:55.130 --> 00:06:59.640
These services are a great way to discover
new artists, but they’re not a great way

00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:00.640
to support them.

00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:05.620
If you really like a band, consider buying
their albums or merch, or watching them perform.

00:07:05.620 --> 00:07:10.050
They get more money that way, and the joy
and fulfillment these artists bring our lives

00:07:10.050 --> 00:07:12.580
is worth paying for.

00:07:12.580 --> 00:07:14.000
And that’s our two cents!

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:18.520
Thanks to our patrons for keeping Two Cents financially healthy.

